<div class="help-content mt-4">
    <section class="mb-4">
        <h1>Yu-Gi-Oh! Advanced Probability Calculator <small class="text-muted">v@(ProjectConstants.Version)</small></h1>
        <p>Calculate the exact probability of opening one or more defined combo routes in your deck.</p>
        <p>The calculator is built around three things: your deck, reusable card categories, and the combos you want to open.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2>Getting started</h2>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Enter your deck or import a <code>.ydk</code> file.</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Create custom categories</strong> for reusable roles; imported cards already expose objective card properties.</li>
            <li><strong>Assign categories to cards.</strong> A card can belong to any number of categories.</li>
            <li><strong>Create combos</strong> using categories, specific cards, or both.</li>
            <li><strong>Set the required minimum and maximum counts.</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Calculate</strong> to see the probability of each combo and the combined probability of opening any active combo.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>You can save the complete setup as a session and load it again later.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2>Categories</h2>
        <p>Categories are reusable labels for cards that can fulfill the same role.</p>
        <p>For example, the included Vanquish Soul / K9 session uses three custom role categories:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>VS Monster</code></li>
            <li><code>VS Starter</code></li>
            <li><code>K9 Starter</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Objective properties such as <code>Attribute: FIRE</code>, <code>Attribute: DARK</code>, and <code>Level 5</code> come from card metadata instead of duplicate user categories. Functional exceptions can be assigned a property manually; for example, Reinforcement of the Army is treated as FIRE, while Chaotic Elements and &quot;A Case for K9&quot; are treated as Level 5 for combo modeling.</p>
        <p>A card can combine several custom roles and properties. Vanquish Soul Razen, for example, is both a <code>VS Monster</code> and a <code>VS Starter</code> and also has the FIRE property.</p>
        <p>Categories and card properties make it possible to describe a route such as <code>K9 Starter + Lv. 5</code> once instead of creating a separate combo for every possible K9 starter.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2>Combos and requirements</h2>
        <p>A combo is satisfied when all of its requirements can be met by the opening hand.</p>
        <p>Use the pencil on a category requirement or OR alternative to add <strong>AND</strong> categories and edit one shared Min/Max. <code>(Orcust AND Monster) Min 2</code> needs two cards that each match both properties. Separate requirements joined by <code>+</code> still need distinct physical copies. Removing conjuncts can collapse the requirement to one category.</p>
        <p>Click <strong>Add OR</strong>, then click a condition or whole OR group in the expanded expression. Enter the alternative with the type and range inputs and choose <strong>Add alternative</strong>. Cancel target selection or alternative authoring to restore your ordinary draft. Remove alternatives with the cross inside each chip. <code>Razen + (Fire OR Dark)</code> means Razen AND either Fire or Dark. Each alternative keeps its own Min and fixed Max or Any; only the selected alternative's bounds apply. Satisfying both counts once, and the combo keeps one result row. You can add several alternatives or several flat OR groups; requirements outside each group are joined by AND.</p>
        <p>Separate positive requirements still need distinct copies across the selected route. <code>Fire (2–2) OR Dark (2–2)</code> needs two Fire or two Dark, not one of each. Repeated references to the exact same target intersect their bounds; use Min to request multiple copies.</p>
        <p>Requirements can reference:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>a category,</li>
            <li>a specific card,</li>
            <li>or a mixture of both.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Imported cards automatically expose objective properties, such as Monster, attribute, level, or Quick-Play Spell, under <strong>Card properties</strong> in combo category selection. These derived properties are read-only; use your own categories for custom roles such as Starter or Extender.</p>
        <p>Each requirement has a minimum and maximum count. Leave <strong>Max</strong> empty for <strong>Any</strong> (the current hand size), or enter a number for a fixed maximum; <code>0</code> remains a fixed zero.</p>
        <p>Separate positive requirements need separate physical card copies. For example, if a card belongs to both <code>VS Starter</code> and <code>Fire</code>, one copy cannot satisfy both parts of <code>VS Starter + Fire</code> by itself.</p>
        <p>Maximums apply to all matching cards in the hand. Setting both the minimum and maximum to <code>0</code> can therefore be used to exclude a card or category from a combo entirely.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2>Deterministic draw effects</h2>
        <p>In an expanded card entry, choose <strong>None</strong>, <strong>Draw 1</strong>, <strong>Draw 2</strong>, or <strong>Draw 3</strong>. With <strong>Once per turn</strong> enabled (the default), the first copy encountered automatically draws that many cards and is consumed; later copies of the same entry stay in hand. Turn it off to let every copy activate and be consumed, including copies drawn by another effect. Separate entries have independent allowances, even when their names match.</p>
        <p>Requirements apply to the final hand. Retained duplicates count toward categories, specific-card requirements, and maximums; <strong>Any</strong> uses the complete final hand size. If a requested draw cannot be completed, that outcome fails. Other outcomes keep their original probability.</p>
        <p><strong>None</strong> makes the card ordinary without deactivating it. Newly imported or explicitly selected real cards in a small curated set receive an initial draw preset. Other imports and selections, and manual free-text cards, start with no effect. Presets are defaults you can change or clear; saved sessions keep your chosen setting. When a draw amount is set, <strong>After drawing</strong> supports None, Remove 1, or Remove 2. <strong>Eligible</strong> supports Any card, one category/property, same-card AND groups, or OR alternatives containing those groups. Use <strong>Add AND</strong> within a filter alternative to require all selected properties on the same card. New Dark World Dealings defaults to Draw 1 / discard 1; Graceful Charity defaults to Draw 3 / discard 2, both without once-per-turn limits. Existing saves do not gain these discard settings. New Allure of Darkness defaults to Draw 2 / non-OPT / remove one DARK card; Radiant Typhoon Vision defaults to Draw 2 / OPT / remove one Quick-Play Spell OR Radiant Typhoon card. Missing eligible material fails the branch without renormalizing other outcomes. Existing saved Allure/Radiant cards keep their earlier settings. Choosing Draw None clears the removal setting and filter.</p>
        <p>In the gear <strong>Calculation settings</strong> menu, <strong>Draw simulation</strong> has two levels of complexity. <strong>Draw effects</strong> enables Basic simulation: fixed draws and once-per-turn limits, ignoring configured pre-draw costs and post-draw removal, filter, and failure settings. The nested <strong>Advanced draw simulation</strong> switch adds stored pre-draw costs and post-draw removal settings and defaults off. Both levels require Draw effects to be on. Turning Draw effects off also turns Advanced off; re-enabling Draw effects returns to Basic simulation. Advanced mode is a runtime preference, not a saved, shared, or recovery session field; loading or importing a deck does not enable it. The separate Storage safety limit remains available in either mode and applies to every calculation path.</p>
        <p>In Advanced mode, each source is consumed, its complete batch is drawn, and its removal happens before the next surviving FIFO candidate activates. The consumed source cannot be removed again, but newly drawn cards can. Only eligible cards enter the removal ranking. Keep priority is: cards matching positive requirements in any active route, then draw cards that can still activate, then unrelated or non-usable cards. Maximum-only requirements do not protect cards. Ties use stable requirement membership and card identity, independently of deck-row order. Discarded candidates never activate.</p>
        <p><strong>Before drawing</strong> can remove one or two eligible cards from hand as an activation cost. The full cost must be available without using the activating copy itself. Otherwise the source stays in hand and does not use its once-per-turn allowance. FIFO candidates that cannot pay are deferred; after another activation finishes, retry from the oldest surviving candidate. A pass without activation ends resolution. Paid costs are not refunded on deck exhaustion. Costs use the same deterministic keep policy as post-draw removals.</p>
        <p>New Trade-In presets remove one Level 8 AND Monster before Draw 2, without OPT. New Orcustrated Return presets remove one (Orcust AND Monster) OR (World Legacy AND Monster) from hand before Draw 2, with OPT. Return's field alternative and Graveyard/discard triggers are not modeled. Existing saves do not gain these presets.</p>
        <p>All individual, group, and total results use the same policy and resulting hands. Changing active routes can change which cards are protected. Random draws are counted exactly for this deterministic policy; this is <strong>not optimal-play simulation</strong>. Discard triggers, optional activation, searches, and other card effects remain unsupported.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2>Reading the results</h2>
        <p>Each active combo gets its own probability.</p>
        <p>Because the same opening hand can satisfy several combos, those percentages may overlap and should not simply be added together.</p>
        <p><strong>Any active combo</strong> is the exact probability that the opening hand satisfies at least one active combo. A hand that satisfies several routes is still counted only once.</p>
        <p>Combos can also be placed into groups. A group result works the same way: it shows the probability of opening at least one active combo in that group.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2>Example: Vanquish Soul / K9</h2>
        <p>The included Vanquish Soul / K9 example models nine underlying routes as eight combo definitions. <code>VS Starter + (Fire OR Dark)</code> combines two starter routes into one OR definition. The routes either reach the full Vanquish Soul setup directly or can make Ripper + Saryuja as a bridge. Half boards are intentionally not counted.</p>
        <p>With all eight combo definitions active, the modeled probability of opening at least one of them is <strong>83.61%</strong>.</p>
        <p>Use <strong>Try It with Example Data</strong> below to load the complete configuration into the calculator.</p>
        <div class="mt-3">
            <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="LoadExampleSession">Try It with Example Data</button>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_errorMessage)) {
                <div class="alert alert-danger mt-2">
                    @_errorMessage
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2>Experimenting with a deck</h2>
        <p>Cards and combos can be temporarily deactivated without deleting them. This is useful for comparing deck choices or removing a route from the calculation.</p>
        <p>You can also reorder cards, categories, combos, and combo groups to keep larger configurations readable.</p>
        <p>New saved sessions use schema 5 for pre-draw costs. Supported older sessions migrate without acquiring costs or presets; schema 4 draw settings retain their meaning. Older applications may reject new saves; ancient readers without schema checks may misinterpret them.</p>
        <p>When you want to keep a setup, save it as a session. Sessions preserve the deck, categories, combo definitions, ordering, and other calculator settings.</p>
        <p>Imported cards show thumbnails automatically as their rows approach the viewport. Expand a card editor for a larger preview using the same image. Artwork is served from retained project-hosted copies and is recovered using the card's external ID when a session is loaded. Missing or unavailable images do not affect calculations. Card images are supplied by <a href="https://ygoprodeck.com/api-guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YGOPRODeck</a>; the calculator's software license does not cover third-party artwork.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2>Credits</h2>
        <p>Created by <a href="https://github.com/SeyTi01" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SeyTi01</a>.</p>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private string? _errorMessage;

    private async Task LoadExampleSession() {
        try {
            string? fileContent = await _fileService.ReadAllTextAsync("sample-data/example_session_state.json");

            if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(fileContent)) {
                _errorMessage = "The example session file is empty";
                StateHasChanged();
                return;
            }

            SessionState session = await _sessionService.LoadSessionAsync(fileContent);
            _pendingSessionService.PendingSession = session;
            NavigationManager.NavigateTo("/", forceLoad: false);
        }
        catch (Exception ex) {
            _errorMessage = $"Failed to load example session: {ex.Message}";
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }
}
